*************************************************
*** COMPILING and INSTALLING QORE FROM SOURCE ***
*************************************************

see README-GIT to learn how to build qore from git sources
see README-WINDOWS for info specific to the windows builds

Build Requirements
------------------
*) a C++ compiler with C++17 support

*) flex 2.5.31 (or greater -- 2.5.4 or before will NOT work, sorry.  flex 2.5.37 or greater is recommended)
qore requires flex 2.5.31+ to build a reentrant parser; flex 2.5.37 or greater is recommended.
CMake will locate flex automatically; to use a non-default flex, set -DFLEX_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/flex when configuring.

*) bison 1.85 (or better, 2.* versions are fine)
qore requires bison 1.85+ to be able to interface properly with the scanner produced by flex 2.5.3*+

*) POSIX threads
OS-level POSIX thread support is required to build qore.

*) PCRE2 (libpcre2-8)
for Perl-Compatible Regular Expressions. Install libpcre2-dev (Debian/Ubuntu), pcre2-devel (RHEL/Fedora), or pcre2-dev (Alpine).
	https://github.com/PCRE2Project/pcre2

*) openssl 0.9.7 or higher (3.0 or better recommended)
	http://www.openssl.org

*) bzlib 1.0.4 or higher

*) zlib 1.1.3 or higher

*) mpfr 2.4.1 or higher - version 3.1+ recommended + note that the gmp library is also required to build mpfr
   	http://www.mpfr.org/
	http://gmplib.org/
This is for the arbitrary-precision numeric type introduced with Qore 0.8.6

*) brotli
	https://github.com/google/brotli
Required for Brotli compression support. Install libbrotli-dev (Debian/Ubuntu), brotli-devel (RHEL/Fedora), or brotli-dev (Alpine).

*) zstd (Zstandard) 1.4 or higher
	https://facebook.github.io/zstd/
Required for Zstandard compression support. Install libzstd-dev (Debian/Ubuntu), libzstd-devel (RHEL/Fedora), or zstd-dev (Alpine).

*) lz4
	https://lz4.github.io/lz4/
Required for LZ4 compression support. Install liblz4-dev (Debian/Ubuntu), lz4-devel (RHEL/Fedora), or lz4-dev (Alpine).

*) nghttp2
	https://nghttp2.org/
Required for HTTP/2 support. Install libnghttp2-dev (Debian/Ubuntu), nghttp2-devel (RHEL/Fedora), or nghttp2-dev (Alpine).

*) ngtcp2 + ngtcp2_crypto_ossl + nghttp3
	https://github.com/ngtcp2/ngtcp2
	https://github.com/ngtcp2/nghttp3
Required for QUIC/HTTP/3 support. The ngtcp2_crypto_ossl backend requires OpenSSL 3.5+ (the QUIC TLS API).
Install libngtcp2-dev and libngtcp2-crypto-ossl-dev and libnghttp3-dev (Debian/Ubuntu),
ngtcp2-devel and ngtcp2-crypto-ossl-devel and nghttp3-devel (RHEL/Fedora),
or ngtcp2-dev and ngtcp2-crypto-ossl-dev and nghttp3-dev (Alpine).

Note: System packages on Ubuntu 24.04 (OpenSSL 3.0) and macOS Homebrew (OpenSSL 3.x) do not provide
the OpenSSL QUIC TLS backend. On these platforms, build ngtcp2 and nghttp3 from source with OpenSSL 3.5+:

    # First, build and install OpenSSL 3.5+ if not already available
    # Then build ngtcp2 with the OpenSSL backend:
    git clone https://github.com/ngtcp2/ngtcp2.git && cd ngtcp2
    git submodule update --init
    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local \
      -DENABLE_OPENSSL=ON -DENABLE_GNUTLS=OFF -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF
    cmake --build build && sudo cmake --install build

    git clone https://github.com/ngtcp2/nghttp3.git && cd nghttp3
    git submodule update --init
    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF
    cmake --build build && sudo cmake --install build

If OpenSSL 3.5+ is installed in a non-standard location (e.g. /opt/openssl-3.5), pass its
location to both the ngtcp2 and Qore builds:

    # ngtcp2 build with custom OpenSSL:
    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local \
      -DENABLE_OPENSSL=ON -DENABLE_GNUTLS=OFF -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF \
      -DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR=/opt/openssl-3.5

    # Qore build with custom OpenSSL:
    cmake .. -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR=/opt/openssl-3.5

*) Rust toolchain with cargo (optional)
	https://rustup.rs/
Optional; only needed for installing the tree-sitter CLI tool when developing the astparser grammar.
Install rust and cargo: rust cargo (RHEL/Fedora), cargo (Debian/Ubuntu), cargo rust (Alpine).
Then install the tree-sitter CLI: cargo install tree-sitter-cli@0.26.5

*) tree-sitter CLI (optional; for astparser grammar development)
	https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/
Optional; only needed when regenerating the Qore grammar parser sources for the astparser module.
Pre-generated parser sources are included in the source tree and are used by the CMake build by default.
The tree-sitter C runtime library is fetched automatically by CMake at build time.
Install with: cargo install tree-sitter-cli@0.26.5

CMAKE Build
-----------

Example from the root source directory:

    mkdir build; cd build
    cmake .. -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr
    make && make docs && sudo make install

OS-Specific Issues
------------------
*) Linux:
there are no particular issues on Linux, this is one of the main development platforms.
Various distributions have been tested: FC3-8, Gentoo, Ubuntu, ARCH, etc

*) FreeBSD:
building from source - for FreeBSD up to 9.1: flex has to be installed from ports
as long as all the prerequisite libraries are installed, cmake should set up Qore for building and installing on FreeBSD without the need for any particular options or environment variables
note that there is a freebsd port of Qore available (http://www.freebsd.org/cgi/cvsweb.cgi/ports/lang/qore)

*) Darwin - OS/X
One of the main development platforms for Qore.  No particular issues with recent versions of OS/X.  Note that there is a port available for Qore in macports (https://trac.macports.org/browser/trunk/dports/lang/qore/Portfile); there are also ports for the development version and for many binary modules

*) Solaris:
Supported with g++ with c++17 support, Oracle Studio CC not tested recently

*) HP-UX
Qore supported HP-UX in the past, but it has not been tested for a few years.
Itanium should be supported better than PA-RISC.

*) Windows
A fully functional version of Qore can now be build for Windows; see README-WINDOWS for more information

CPU Support
-----------
*) gcc with i386, x86_64, ppc, sparc32, itanium, aCC with itanium, CC (SunPro or Sun Studio CC) with i386, x86_64, sparc32: fast inline assembly atomic operations are supported for reference counting, as well as a SMP cache invalidation optimization for temporary objects (temporary objects do not require a cache invalidation);
*) CPU stack guard is working on all above combinations and with g++ and aCC with PA-RISC as well
*) all others: a pthread mutex is used to ensure atomicity for reference counting.
The cache invalidation optimization is not safe on platforms without an atomic reference counting implementation, therefore is not implemented for these platforms

